      "disclosureOnFullTurn": false,
      "answer": "To reverse a linked list in place, you iterate through the list while reassigning each node's pointer to the previous node. You maintain three pointers\u2014`prev`, `current`, and `next`\u2014to track the nodes as you disconnect and reconnect them, ensuring you don't lose the rest of the list during the process.\n\nHere is a common implementation in Python:\n\n```python\ndef reverse_list(head):\n    prev = None\n    current = head\n    while current:\n        next_node = current.next\n        current.next = prev\n        prev = current\n        current = next_node\n    return prev\n```",
